Insuperlex is one of [[Aut]]'s siblings, called the Mountain. He is the obstacle set before a hero, the trials and tribulations a protagonist must overcome to reach a goal, and he clashes with [[Divinity/The Narrative/Menhir|the Menhir]] the same way [[Aut]] clashes with [[Divinity/The Narrative/Renge|Renge]]. Those who serve him get nothing but challenges, and of all the narrative siblings he is held to be the worst master. He has lately taken an interest in the Party's pilgrimage, and the servants of his siblings now assume all five of them are watching the voyage.

The Menhir he clashed with is dead, killed by the Party with the weapon the Menhir itself had put in their hands, and the counterpart that checked him is gone. He showed himself to the Party in a white void after they killed [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/Esoterica|Gremory]], half of him visible at a time as he turned, and said that though he was angry with them he would not act against them. Their strider had not broken up on the crossing because of his influence: he had argued they should be stranded for the greater challenge of fighting their way back, out of respect for the dying wish of his sibling. He was not like [[Aut]], he said, and would not silently direct them. He offered a road home instead: if two more of the [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/index|Ashen Pact]] were killed, he and a being known well to him would open a way back into [[Geography/Færrin/index|Færrin]]. He said there were four routes into Færrin and only four, and his was by far the easiest; that no Ashen Pact had passed the wall in its current form and none ever would, and that any Ashen Pact they sought would stay out here. He could stay only a little while, and was gone.
